window.LiveFitArticleById = id => window.LiveFitArticles.find(a=>a.id===id) || window.LiveFitArticles[0];

window.LiveFitBuildArticle = function(a){
 const list=a.starter.map(x=>'<li>'+x+'</li>').join('');
 const section=(title,body)=>'<section><h2>'+title+'</h2>'+body+'</section>';
 const p=t=>'<p>'+t+'</p>';
 let html='';
 html+=section('1. Start with the goal that matters now',
   p('The useful question behind <strong>'+a.title+'</strong> is not “What is the perfect program?” It is “What can I repeat long enough to improve?” For adults in '+a.audience+', the best plan usually combines enough challenge to create adaptation with enough restraint to recover and return. That means choosing a small number of movements, setting a realistic weekly rhythm, and judging success by consistency rather than by a single hard workout.')+
   p('This guide focuses on '+a.focus+'. Those pieces work together. Strength without recovery becomes difficult to sustain. Cardio without strength can leave important capacities undertrained. Mobility without strength may improve how a position feels but not how well you control it. Nutrition without a practical routine can become another short-lived project. The goal is an integrated plan that fits a real schedule and a real body.')+
   p('If you are returning after a long layoff, managing a chronic condition, or noticing symptoms that are new, severe, or worsening, use this article as general education rather than personal medical advice. A clinician or qualified professional can help you adapt exercise when there are medical restrictions, unexplained symptoms, recent surgery, or significant pain.'));
 html+=section('2. What tends to change with age — and what does not',
   p('Aging can change recovery speed, muscle mass, connective-tissue tolerance, balance, confidence, sleep quality, and the amount of activity built into daily life. None of those changes means improvement is off the table. They simply make programming quality more important. You may benefit from a longer warm-up, fewer all-out sets, more attention to technique, and a little more time between very demanding sessions.')+
   p('The basic training principles still hold. Muscles respond to progressive resistance. Aerobic fitness responds to repeated cardiovascular work. Balance improves with practice. Skills improve when they are practiced often enough to become familiar. The body still responds to stress and recovery; the useful stress just needs to match your current capacity.')+
   p('This is why copying the routine of a much younger athlete can be a poor fit even when the exercises themselves are fine. The difference is often dose: how much, how hard, how often, and how quickly the workload increases. A mature plan uses the same fundamentals with better judgment.'));
 html+=section('3. Build around a small movement menu',
   p('A simple menu is easier to learn, track, and progress. For this topic, a useful starting set is:')+'<ul>'+list+'</ul>'+
   p('You do not need every exercise in every session. The point is to cover useful movement patterns across the week. Choose versions that feel stable, allow controlled repetitions, and can be adjusted in small steps. A chair, bench, wall, resistance band, dumbbell, or railing can make an exercise more accessible without making it less valuable.')+
   p('When a movement is uncomfortable, first change range of motion, load, stance, grip, or tempo before deciding that the entire pattern is off limits. Sharp pain, sudden instability, chest discomfort, faintness, or other concerning symptoms are different; those deserve appropriate medical attention rather than an exercise workaround.'));
 html+='<div class="ad-art-marker" aria-hidden="true"><span>◆</span></div>';
 html+=section('4. Use effort you can repeat',
   p('Most productive training does not require reaching absolute failure. A practical approach is to finish many sets feeling that you could have performed another two or three good repetitions. That leaves enough effort in the set to stimulate progress while limiting the breakdown in technique and fatigue that can make the next session harder to recover from.')+
   p('On cardio days, the same idea applies. Easy work should actually feel easy. Moderate work should be controlled. Hard intervals can be useful, but they should be a small part of the week for most recreational adults. The talk test is a simple guide: during easier aerobic work, you should generally be able to speak in complete sentences. During harder intervals, speech becomes shorter.')+
   p('The right intensity is the one that allows a pattern of successful weeks. One spectacular session followed by four inactive days usually produces less progress than three or four manageable sessions completed consistently.'));
 html+=section('5. Progress one variable at a time',
   p('Progress does not have to mean adding weight every workout. You can add a repetition, improve range of motion, slow the lowering phase, improve control, add a set, shorten a rest period slightly, walk a little farther, or repeat the same workload with less effort. These are all forms of progress.')+
   p('A useful rule is to make the smallest change that keeps the work challenging. Large jumps create unnecessary soreness and can make technique deteriorate. Small changes also make it easier to identify what your body tolerates well. If you add weight and volume and frequency all at once, it becomes hard to know which change caused a problem.')+
   p('Every few weeks, review your training rather than simply pushing forward. Ask whether your performance, energy, soreness, sleep, and motivation are moving in the right direction. If not, reduce the workload slightly and rebuild.'));
 html+=section('6. Recovery is part of the program',
   p('Recovery is not passive laziness. It is the period in which you adapt to training. Sleep, food, hydration, stress, medication, illness, and daily responsibilities can all affect how recovered you feel. Adults with busy schedules often need to manage training around life rather than pretending life will stay out of the way.')+
   p('Use lighter days on purpose. Walking, gentle mobility, easy cycling, or simple household movement can help you stay active without turning every day into a test. If soreness changes how you walk or move, that is usually a sign that the previous session was more than you needed.')+
   p('A sustainable week has room for imperfect sleep, travel, family demands, and occasional low-energy days. The plan should bend before it breaks.'));
 html+=section('7. Nutrition should support the training',
   p('Food does not need to become complicated because you are exercising. Build meals around recognizable foods, include a useful protein source, add produce when practical, choose carbohydrate portions that fit your activity, and include fats in reasonable amounts. The exact balance can vary by preference, culture, medical needs, and goals.')+
   p('For adults trying to preserve or build muscle, spreading protein across the day can be easier than trying to catch up at dinner. For weight management, total energy intake still matters, but extreme restriction can make training, sleep, and adherence harder. A moderate approach is usually easier to repeat.')+
   p('Hydration is also easy to underestimate, especially in hot weather, during longer sessions, or when medications affect fluid balance. Use thirst, urine color, climate, and your clinician’s advice if you have medical fluid restrictions.'));
 html+='<div class="ad-art-marker" aria-hidden="true"><span>◇</span></div>';
 html+=section('8. A practical weekly structure',
   p('A simple week might include two or three strength sessions, two or more purposeful walks or cardio sessions, and brief mobility or balance work on several days. The exact schedule should reflect your current level. Someone beginning from very little activity can improve with much less than someone who has trained for years.')+
   p('Strength sessions can be full-body so that missing one day does not leave an entire body region untrained. Cardio can be divided between comfortable steady work and occasional short intervals. Mobility can be attached to warm-ups, cooldowns, or movement breaks rather than requiring a separate hour.')+
   p('The best schedule is not the one that looks most impressive on paper. It is the one you can execute during an ordinary week.'));
 html+=section('9. Use a two-level plan for difficult days',
   p('Create a normal session and a minimum session. The normal session is what you do when time and energy are available. The minimum session is what you do when the day goes sideways. It might be ten minutes of walking, one set of the major strength movements, or a short mobility sequence.')+
   p('This prevents the all-or-nothing pattern in which anything less than a complete workout feels like failure. The minimum session keeps the habit alive and makes it easier to return to the normal plan the next day or later in the week.')+
   p('Consistency is not perfect attendance. It is the ability to resume quickly after interruptions.'));
 html+=section('10. Track what is useful',
   p('A training log can be simple: exercise, weight or resistance, repetitions, sets, walking time or distance, and a brief note about effort. You do not need to measure everything. Track enough to see whether the work is becoming easier, heavier, longer, or more controlled over time.')+
   p('Also track outcomes that matter in daily life. Stairs may feel easier. You may carry groceries with less effort, rise from a chair more smoothly, sleep better after active days, or feel more confident walking on uneven ground. Those changes are meaningful even if a scale or wearable moves slowly.')+
   p('Use data as feedback, not as a judgment. A bad day is information. A good week is information. Adjust the plan based on patterns, not on one isolated number.'));
 html+=section('11. Your first four weeks',
   p('Week one should feel almost too manageable. Learn the movements and establish a rhythm. In week two, repeat the same structure and make one or two small improvements. In week three, add a modest amount of work only where recovery is good. In week four, either continue progressing or hold steady and consolidate.')+
   p('You do not need a dramatic transformation in four weeks. The first month is about proving that the plan fits. Once you have evidence that you can recover from it and repeat it, progression becomes much easier to manage.')+
   '<div class="article-checklist"><label><input type="checkbox"> I chose realistic training days.</label><label><input type="checkbox"> I selected a minimum-session option.</label><label><input type="checkbox"> I know which movements I will repeat.</label><label><input type="checkbox"> I will progress one variable at a time.</label></div>');
 html+=section('12. When to adjust or get help',
   p('Exercise should challenge you, but it should not require you to ignore warning signs. Stop and seek appropriate medical care for chest pain or pressure, fainting, severe shortness of breath that is unusual for you, sudden neurological symptoms, or other emergency concerns. Persistent or worsening musculoskeletal pain also deserves assessment rather than repeated training through it.')+
   p('If your plan consistently leaves you exhausted, unusually sore, or unable to complete normal daily tasks, reduce the dose. If it feels easy for several weeks and performance is stable, add a small challenge. If you are unsure how to adapt exercises around surgery, osteoporosis, heart disease, significant arthritis, neurological conditions, or other medical issues, a clinician or appropriately qualified exercise professional can help.')+
   p('The long-term target is not to win one workout. It is to build enough strength, endurance, mobility, and confidence that movement remains part of your life. Start where you are, make the next sensible adjustment, and keep going.'));
 html+='<div class="article-caution"><strong>LiveFit note:</strong> This article provides general fitness and wellness education. It is not a diagnosis or individualized medical treatment plan.</div>';
 return html;
};

